Glossarion overlay of flet-dev/mobile-forge recipes/llama-cpp-python/patches/mobile.patch
(0fadbfab0d63d11164030dfddc955ed980e32e2c, BSD-3-Clause, see ../../LICENSE.mobile-forge):
upstream's five changes below, unchanged, plus a sixth one for the vision build (LLAVA_BUILD=ON)
and a seventh one for the runtime CPU variants of the Android arm64 build:

6. Drops SONAME versioning from libmtmd too (single unversioned libmtmd, like the others).

7. Runtime CPU-variant selection (meta.yaml builds Android arm64 with GGML_BACKEND_DL=ON and
   GGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS=ON):
   - ggml/src/CMakeLists.txt: the Android variant list is cut to the four NEON feature sets
     android_armv8.0_1 (baseline), android_armv8.2_1 (dotprod), android_armv8.2_2 (dotprod, fp16)
     and android_armv8.6_1 (dotprod, fp16, i8mm); no SVE / SVE2 / SME variants. The option
     GLOSSARION_ANDROID_CPU_VARIANTS builds the same set on Linux arm64 (the CI check).
   - _ctypes_extensions.py: after preloading libggml, register_cpu_backend() asks every variant
     library for its ggml_backend_score() (the feature probe; 0 = the CPU lacks a feature) and
     registers the best with ggml_backend_load(), loading by bare soname from the APK's jniLibs
     where no file exists (ggml's own ggml_backend_load_all() only scans the executable's
     directory and the cwd). CPU_BACKEND_VARIANT names the registered variant ("builtin" when
     libggml links the CPU backend itself: x86_64, iOS).

Upstream's description:

It does five things:

1. Stops llama-cpp-python's CMakeLists from FORCE-enabling GGML_METAL on all
   Apple platforms (it would override our -DGGML_METAL=OFF for the iOS cross
   build, which has no Metal toolchain).
2. Skips building llama.cpp's unused `common` helper lib (~5 MB).
3. Drops SONAME versioning from the shipped libs (ggml-base/ggml/ggml-cpu/
   llama) so the wheel carries single unversioned files instead of a
   lib*.dylib -> .0 -> .0.15.3 symlink triplet that forge dereferences into
   three copies (and three colliding frameworks on iOS).
4. Teaches the ctypes loader to load the bundled libs on iOS, where serious-python
   relocates each dylib into a code-signed <name>.framework and leaves a
   lib<name>.fwork TEXT marker (its content is the app-relative framework-binary
   path). The loader now RESOLVES that marker to the real framework binary (as
   CPython's AppleFrameworkLoader does — join the marker text above the wheel dir)
   before ctypes.CDLL, instead of trying to dlopen the text marker itself; and it
   preloads the ggml chain in dependency order (ggml-base -> ggml-cpu -> ggml ->
   llama) with RTLD_GLOBAL. serious-python leaves the dylibs' install-ids
   unchanged (@rpath/lib<name>.dylib), so a preloaded framework satisfies the next
   lib's sibling @rpath reference by install-id. Also handles sys.platform == "ios"
   (flet's 3.13+).
5. Android/Flet 0.86: site-packages ships as a zip and serious-python relocates
   the bundled libs (libllama/libggml*) into the APK's jniLibs, so the loader's
   Path.exists() probes against the in-zip base_path all miss. Add a bare-soname
   fallback (ctypes.CDLL("lib<name>.so")) for both the dependency preload and the
   main load, so the Android linker resolves them from jniLibs.

